1. Sprint Planning & Backlog Refinement
Sprint Planning Assistant: 
• "Act as an experienced Scrum Master. Help me prepare for sprint planning by reviewing the following backlog items: 
[paste items]. Organize them into priorities, identify dependencies, suggest sprint goals, and highlight risks or missing information."


Sprint Goal Prompt (FOCUS Model):
• "Act as an Agile Coach. 
Use the FOCUS model (Fun, Outcome-oriented, Collaborative, Ultimate, Singular) to help our team break down our quarterly goals into a short-term, compelling sprint goal based on these items: [paste backlog items]."

Refine User Stories & Acceptance Criteria:
• "Help me refine user stories by generating a clear Definition of Done and step-by-step acceptance criteria for this feature request: [paste user story]."


2. Daily Standups & Impediment Removal
• Removing Impediments Step-by-Step:"Act as a facilitator. 
Request a step-by-step resolution plan for this team blocker: [describe blocker]. 
Identify potential focus group members, facilitation methods, and collaboration tools to resolve it in under 48 hours."

Categorize Daily Blockers:
"Analyze these daily update notes: [paste updates]. 
Categorize the impediments into team-level (process/tooling) versus organization-level (cross-team/political) and flag delivery risks."


3. Retrospectives & Continuous Improvement
• Retrospective Theme Extraction:"Read the following raw feedback from our last retrospective: [paste feedback text]. 
  Extract underlying patterns and recurring themes—not individual complaints—that appear two or more times."
• Ishikawa / Root-Cause Analysis:"Act as an Agile Coach. Convert this retrospective feedback [paste feedback] 
  into structured problem statements and generate a feed of categories to build an Ishikawa (fishbone) root-cause diagram."

4. Metrics & Stakeholder Reporting
• Sprint Metrics & Predictability Analysis:"Act as an experienced Agile Coach. 
  Analyze the following sprint metrics: Velocity [e.g., 42, 38, 35, 31], Carry-over Stories [4], Defects [12], Team Capacity [85%]. Identify root causes, delivery risks, and recommend 3 practical actions for the Scrum Master and Product Owner to improve predictability without increasing team workload."
• Stakeholder Scope Change Update:"Draft a clear, transparent message to stakeholders explaining the reasons behind a scope change for [project/feature], 
  keeping everyone aligned and managing expectations."



Best Practices for AI Prompting in Scrum

• Set the Role: Always tell the AI to act as a Scrum Master, Agile Coach, or Product Manager first.
• Provide Context: Feed the AI real sprint data, team capacity, or past examples so the output matches your team's style.
• Use Meta-Prompts: If stuck, ask the AI: "Help me rewrite this prompt to get a better output".
• Protect Data: Avoid pasting sensitive company Intellectual Property (IP) or personal data into public LLMs. 
  AI handles the math and text—you still handle the human coaching, trust, and team conversations.


